### What is an algebraic data type?

An algebraic data type has two building blocks. A product type is a bundle of fields, like a struct. In Java a `record` is exactly this. A sum type says a value is exactly one of several alternatives. Combining sums of products is what "algebraic" means: the set of possible values is built by adding up alternatives, each of which multiplies together its fields.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Sum type: one of these three alternatives
public sealed interface PaymentResult
        permits PaymentResult.Success, PaymentResult.Failure, PaymentResult.Pending {

    // Product type: bundles amount
    record Success(BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentResult {}

    // Product type: bundles reason
    record Failure(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}

    // Product type: bundles expectedDate
    record Pending(String expectedDate) implements PaymentResult {}
}
```

### How do sealed types deliver the sum?

A `sealed interface` with a `permits` clause declares that a value is exactly one of the listed variants. The set is closed, so no other variant can exist.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Closed set: only these three outcomes exist
public sealed interface TransferOutcome {
    record Completed(String txnId, BigDecimal amount) implements TransferOutcome {}
    record Rejected(String reason) implements TransferOutcome {}
    record Queued(String queueId, String expectedTime) implements TransferOutcome {}
}
```

### How do records deliver the product?

Each record bundles its variant's own fields. Records are implicitly `final`, so they satisfy the sealed-subtype rule automatically. They are immutable and transparent, so the compiler knows their components.

```java
// Each record is a product: bundled named components
record Completed(String txnId, BigDecimal amount)
        implements TransferOutcome {
    // Canonical constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode, toString
    // all generated automatically
}
```

### How does exhaustive switch consume the ADT?

A `switch` over the sealed type with a case per variant is checked by the compiler for completeness. No `default` is needed, and adding a new variant breaks every non-exhaustive switch.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransferOutcome;
import com.bank.model.TransferOutcome.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferProcessor {

    public String describeOutcome(TransferOutcome outcome) {
        return switch (outcome) {
            case Completed c -> "Transfer " + c.txnId()
                    + " completed: " + c.amount() + " INR";
            case Rejected r -> "Transfer rejected: " + r.reason();
            case Queued q -> "Transfer queued (" + q.queueId()
                    + "), expected at " + q.expectedTime();
            // No default: compiler verifies exhaustiveness
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransferProcessor processor = new TransferProcessor();
        System.out.println(processor.describeOutcome(
                new Completed("TXN-5001", new BigDecimal("75000"))));
        System.out.println(processor.describeOutcome(
                new Rejected("Insufficient funds")));
        System.out.println(processor.describeOutcome(
                new Queued("Q-789", "14:30 IST")));
    }
}
```

```text
Transfer TXN-5001 completed: 75000 INR
Transfer rejected: Insufficient funds
Transfer queued (Q-789), expected at 14:30 IST
```

### How do ADTs make illegal states unrepresentable?

With the old approach of one class with a status enum plus nullable fields, you could accidentally have a success that also had a failure reason set. With an ADT, a `Success` has no `reason` field and a `Failure` has no `amount` field. The invalid combination cannot be constructed.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// OLD approach: illegal states are representable
class OldPaymentResult {
    String status; // "SUCCESS", "FAILURE", "PENDING"
    BigDecimal amount; // null for failure?
    String reason; // null for success?
    // BUG: status="SUCCESS" but reason is set
}

// NEW approach: illegal states are impossible
// sealed interface PaymentResult
//   permits Success(amount), Failure(reason), Pending(date)
// Success has no reason field. Failure has no amount field.
```

### How do ADTs compose recursively?

Sealed-plus-records ADTs compose recursively for trees and expression grammars. This is the classic interpreter pattern done type-safely.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface FeeExpr {
    record Literal(BigDecimal value) implements FeeExpr {}
    record Add(FeeExpr left, FeeExpr right) implements FeeExpr {}
    record Multiply(FeeExpr left, FeeExpr right) implements FeeExpr {}
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.FeeExpr;
import com.bank.model.FeeExpr.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FeeEvaluator {

    public BigDecimal evaluate(FeeExpr expr) {
        return switch (expr) {
            case Literal l -> l.value();
            case Add a -> evaluate(a.left()).add(evaluate(a.right()));
            case Multiply m -> evaluate(m.left()).multiply(evaluate(m.right()));
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // fee = (500 + 200) * 1.18 (GST)
        FeeExpr fee = new Multiply(
                new Add(new Literal(new BigDecimal("500")),
                        new Literal(new BigDecimal("200"))),
                new Literal(new BigDecimal("1.18"))
        );

        FeeEvaluator evaluator = new FeeEvaluator();
        System.out.println("Fee with GST: " + evaluator.evaluate(fee) + " INR");
    }
}
```

```text
Fee with GST: 826.00 INR
```

### How is this different from the old enum-plus-class approach?

| Feature | Enum + Class | Sealed + Records (ADT) |
|---|---|---|
| Variant-specific fields | Nullable, shared | Per-variant, non-nullable |
| Illegal states | Possible | Impossible |
| Exhaustive switch | Manual (default needed) | Compiler-checked |
| Pattern deconstruction | Not available | Record patterns |
| Immutability | Manual | Automatic (records) |

### What about thread safety?

Records are immutable by default, making ADT values inherently thread-safe. You can pass a `PaymentResult` across threads without synchronization, and no thread can mutate it.

```java
// Records are immutable: safe to share across threads
PaymentResult result = new PaymentResult.Success(new BigDecimal("75000"));
// Pass to any thread: no synchronization needed
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

ADTs encourage total functions: a function that maps every variant to a result, verified exhaustive by the compiler. This is safer than scattered `instanceof` chains. In a bank, regulatory changes that add a new state are caught at compile time everywhere they must be handled. ADTs also pair naturally with immutable records for auditable, side-effect-free domain modelling.
